# **`perish` is the consequence `hungry` does not carry.** A trait says what a thing may do; what
# happens to a thing that did not is a step of the turn, with a name a player can read.
#
# **It takes the row rather than one of them**, which is what a `remove` clause naming no quantity
# means: everyone still hungry after upkeep goes at once. **So its repetition fires once and then
# runs out**, and that is not what the repetition is for.
#
# **It repeats because *as many times as it can* is zero**, and a turn has to end in a world with
# no citizens in it. A bare `remove` refuses when nothing matches, which is right for a player's
# command and wrong for a step the turn fires whatever the world holds.
#
# **So every step of the turn is a no-op when there is nothing to do**, by one of three routes -
# `put` assigns nothing, `keep` finds nothing over, and a repetition fires zero times.
#
# **The quantity it used to name was decoration and the sweep said so.** With `repeats` the rule
# reaches the same world one at a time or all at once, so `{literal ... column:132 value:1}` could
# be deleted and nothing failed.

{rule id:9 name:perish}

{input id:16 rule:9 seq:1 name:where of:48}

