# **`toil` is a citizen spending its `laboring` to make a labour**, and it is `upkeep`'s mirror:
# one takes a food and gives back a citizen that has eaten, this takes nothing and gives back a
# citizen that has worked, plus the labour it made.
#
# **It is the player's and not the turn's.** Sean, 2026-09-20: *I was expecting citizens to generate
# labor during a regular turn.* So it takes its place as an argument like every other command, and
# it is a root of the tree rather than a step of `end-turn` - which is what makes working a choice
# rather than something that happens to you.
#
# **One command works a whole place**, because it repeats: it fires once per citizen that still has
# its `laboring`, and stops when none has. **A citizen produces one labour per turn**, which is the
# `quantity:1` on the clause that makes it. Sean, 2026-09-20: *in the future I will want to be able
# to change how much labor a single citizen can produce per turn* - and when that day comes the
# number stops being a literal and becomes a rate, the way `{consumes kind:scout what:berth} -> 1`
# already is. `backlog.md` carries it.
#
# **It reads the citizen it gives back, rather than stating one.** A citizen carries three traits,
# and an `add` must name every column - so writing `hungry:1 bearing:1` would be right only because
# the turn restores them last. The `require` is there to be read from, which is what `work` does
# with a deposit's density and `upkeep` with a parent's bearing.

{rule id:11 name:toil}

