# **`launch` builds an ark and puts it in orbit, and that is one action.** Sean, 2026-09-20:
# *building/launching an ark will be one action, removing the resources required to build an ark
# from the surface and adding an ark to orbit.* **Nothing crosses between layers**, so this is not a
# journey with two halves - it is a transformation whose inputs are on the ground and whose output
# is above it.
#
# **The orbit is worked out and not named.** The player says which surface place is building, the
# rule reads that place's territory, and the orbit above is the one place with that territory and
# that layer - found by matching rather than chosen. `spec/console.md` has the phrase: **a place
# worked out from another is not open.**
#
# **A labour to assemble it, a metal to build it, an energy to launch it.** Sean: *tuning is going
# to be separate, for now I am making everything cheap.* Each is one, and each is taken from the
# ground rather than from the sky.
#
# **Everything made arrives at full capability**, which is Sean's rule rather than this rule's.
# Sean, 2026-09-20: *Generally i want things created with full capability.* So a built extractor may
# be worked the turn it is built, a launched ark may move and gather, and a citizen off an ark or
# out of a breeding may labour and bear.
#
# **`hungry` is the one that cannot say it that way**, and that is the whole of why a rename to
# `fed` keeps coming up: a capacity at full is `1` and an obligation at full is `0`, so *everything
# starts at one* is true of five columns and false of the sixth. **The polarity does not go away
# under the other name**; it moves to the turn, which restores a capacity and imposes an obligation
# with the one word `refresh`.
#
# **It names no kind, and one day it will.** Only an ark launches today; a second launchable kind
# turns the rule's output into an argument, the way `discard-disorder` grew one the day a second
# family lay loose.

{rule id:13 name:launch}

