## What an ark becomes when it comes down

# **Sean, 2026-09-20**, whole: *ark can deploy to the surface, which entails destruction of the ark,
# along with creation of 1 metal extractor, 1 food extractor, and 2 citizens.*
#
# **The ark is spent before anything is made**, which is the clause order and is deliberate. Sean:
# *it is legal to deploy an ark anywhere, individual rules may fail but the deployment succeeds.*
# So a deployment onto barren ground still costs the ark, and
# `a-deployment-with-nowhere-to-mine-still-costs-the-ark` is what says so.
#
# **The two extractors are soft and the two citizens are not.** An extractor stands in a deposit and
# there may be none; nothing gives room for a citizen, so a citizen can never be short - **and it is
# the absence of a capacity row that says that**, rather than anything written here.
#
# **The ground is found by matching, the same way `launch` finds the orbit.** An orbit names the
# territory it belongs to, and the surface of that territory is the place whose `of` is the same -
# so the equality is a pattern and never a comparison.
#
# **Nothing crosses a layer.** An ark in orbit is removed and what it becomes is added to the
# ground; `nothing-moves-between-the-layers` is the rule that makes this a transformation rather
# than a journey.

{rule id:14 name:deploy}

